-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. Wildlife Safari Tour
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka''s best national parks in one route. Yala for leopards, Udawalawe for elephants, and Minneriya for the great elephant gathering. Private jeep, expert tracker, and a vehicle that waits for the sightings, not the clock.',
    intro = 'This isn''t a zoo tour. You''re heading into dry-zone national parks where animals move wild and free. Over seven days, you''ll hit three distinct habitats: Yala''s scrub jungle for leopards, Udawalawe''s open grassland for elephants, and Minneriya''s reservoir edge for the famous elephant gathering. Between safaris, you stay at lodges near the park gates so you can be first in line at dawn. That matters — the best sightings happen in the first two hours after sunrise, before the heat drives everything into cover. Your vehicle is a private safari jeep with an open top for photography and a tracker who reads the bush better than most people read street signs.',
    highlights = 'Dawn at Yala when the mist hangs over the scrub and a leopard silhouette appears on a rocky outcrop. Udawalawe at 7am when a herd of 40 elephants crosses the road in front of your jeep. Minneriya between June and September when up to 300 elephants gather around the drying reservoir. Bundala for birders — flamingos, painted storks, and half a dozen raptor species. The elephant transit home at Udawalawe where orphaned calves are rehabilitated before release.',
    pricing_notes = 'Safari costs add up. Budget USD 50-80 per safari for a shared jeep, USD 120-180 for a private one. Park entry fees: USD 25-35 per person per park. With 5-6 safaris over 7 days, expect USD 300-500 per person in wildlife costs. Accommodation near parks runs USD 80-200/night. The private vehicle with driver is separate from the safari jeeps — your car drops you at the lodge, and the jeep picks you up from the lodge for each safari.',
    vehicle_options = 'Your main vehicle is a standard sedan or minivan for inter-park transfers. Safari jeeps are arranged locally at each park. For wildlife photographers, we recommend the private jeep option — you control the positioning, and the tracker can respond to sightings without coordinating with other jeeps.',
    cta_text = 'Leopards, elephants, and the best wildlife in Sri Lanka. Send your dates and we''ll build a safari route around the best sighting seasons.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Which park is best for leopards?","a":"Yala National Park, Block 1. The density is among the highest in Asia. Peak visibility is February-July."},{"q":"Can I combine safaris on the same day?","a":"Not recommended. Each safari is 3-4 hours, and the parks are 1-3 hours apart. One per day is the realistic pace."},{"q":"What should I wear?","a":"Neutral colours (khaki, olive, beige). No bright whites or reds. Closed shoes, a hat, sunscreen, and a light jacket for the morning chill."},{"q":"Are the safari jeeps safe?","a":"Licensed park jeeps are safe with seat belts and roll bars. Book through your hotel or a recommended operator."}]'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-wildlife-safari-tour'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. Classic Sri Lanka Private Tour
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'The route that covers everything a first-timer should see. Sigiriya, Kandy, tea country, safari, and a south coast beach finish. Nine days of Sri Lanka''s best.',
    intro = 'The classic island circuit. Sigiriya''s rock fortress, Kandy''s temple of the tooth, the tea plantations of Nuwara Eliya, an elephant safari, and a south coast beach to decompress at the end. This is the tour we recommend to anyone visiting Sri Lanka for the first time. It''s not trying to be clever or niche — it covers the major regions in a logical loop, with no single-night stays and manageable 3-4 hour drives between stops. The private vehicle stays with you the whole way, so you''re not arranging separate transport for each leg. Your driver doubles as a guide who knows the best roadside stops, the cleanest restrooms, and the roti places that don''t make tourists sick.',
    highlights = 'Climbing Sigiriya at 7am before the bus crowds arrive and baking on the summit by 9. The Kandy-to-Nuwara Eliya drive through winding tea plantations — the landscape changes from tropical to temperate in two hours. A morning safari at Minneriya where elephants cross the road in front of your jeep. Bentota beach on the final day, doing absolutely nothing. The train ride from Kandy to Ella if you want the famous photo — but honestly, your private car is more comfortable and you can stop at waterfalls.',
    pricing_notes = 'Nine days of mid-range accommodation: roughly USD 700-1,000 total. The Bentota beach hotel will be your priciest night at USD 120-200. Safari jeep + park entry around USD 70-90 per person. Train is optional and cheap (USD 5-10). Meals at local restaurants run USD 5-15 per person. The private vehicle and chauffeur are baked into the package — no surge pricing, no meter.',
    vehicle_options = 'A standard sedan (Toyota Axio or similar) works for two passengers. For three or more, upgrade to a minivan (Toyota KDH or Nissan Urvan). The Kandy-to-Nuwara Eliya mountain roads are winding — the minivan''s suspension handles better on rough patches.',
    cta_text = 'Your first Sri Lanka trip deserves the full circuit. Send your dates and we''ll set up the classic nine-day route.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Is this good for families?","a":"Works for families with kids over 8. Younger children may struggle with the Sigiriya climb and the longer drives. Ask us about the family-modified version."},{"q":"Can I swap Bentota for a different beach?","a":"Sure. Hikkaduwa has better snorkelling. Mirissa has whale watching. Tangalle is quieter. Let us know your preference."},{"q":"What time of year works best?","a":"December-April for the south coast leg. May-September still works but the beaches get rougher surf and occasional rain."}]'
WHERE slug = 'classic-sri-lanka-private-tour-package'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. 10-Day Itinerary Package
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'Ten days that cover the Cultural Triangle, hill country, wildlife, and coast. No corners cut, no filler days. Sri Lanka''s greatest hits in a tight package.',
    intro = 'Ten days is the sweet spot for a Sri Lanka trip. You cover every major region without feeling like you''re racing. Culture at Sigiriya and Polonnaruwa, history in Kandy, cool air in the tea hills, a safari in Udawalawe, and two proper beach days on the south coast. The route goes: airport to Sigiriya, Sigiriya to Kandy, Kandy to Nuwara Eliya, Nuwara Eliya to Ella, Ella to Udawalawe, Udawalawe to Galle, Galle to airport. Seven stops over ten days means you''re changing hotels every 1-2 nights, but the drives are manageable. The longest stretch is the Ella-to-Udawalawe drive at 4 hours on winding roads — not fun, but the safari the next morning makes it worth it.',
    highlights = 'Polonnaruwa at 8am on a bicycle, weaving between ruined palaces and buddha statues with the site almost to yourself. The train ride from Kandy to Nuwara Eliya — take the 2-hour scenic stretch, skip the full journey. Udawalawe at 7am watching elephants cross the road. Galle Fort at sunset, walking the ramparts with a king coconut in hand. The beach clubs at Unawatuna if you want a DJ with your sand. The quiet coves of Hiriketiya if you don''t.',
    pricing_notes = 'Ten nights of mid-range accommodation: USD 800-1,200. Safari at Udawalawe: USD 60-80 per person including park fees. Sigiriya entry: USD 30. Polonnaruwa entry: USD 25. Train ticket (scenic stretch): USD 5-10. Meals: USD 5-15 per person at local spots. Your biggest saving is the private vehicle — no taxi bartering, no tuk-tuk negotiations.',
    vehicle_options = 'Standard sedan for 1-2 passengers. Minivan for 3-5. The Ella-to-Udawalawe road is winding and occasionally potholed — the minivan''s extra suspension travel helps. If you get carsick easily, sit in the front passenger seat and carry ginger tablets.',
    cta_text = 'Ten days is the perfect first trip length. Send your travel window and we''ll map the full circuit.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Can I add more beach days?","a":"Swap one of the Ella or Kandy nights for an extra night in Galle or Mirissa. Just know you''ll lose a hill-country day."},{"q":"Is this tour too rushed?","a":"It''s compact but not crazy. The longest drive is 4 hours. You change hotels every 1-2 nights, which is normal for a Sri Lanka circuit. If you want fewer moves, go for the 14-day version."},{"q":"What if I don''t want to climb Sigiriya?","a":"Some people skip the climb and visit Pidurangala instead — cheaper, fewer tourists, and an incredible view of Sigiriya from the side. We can swap it."}]'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-10-day-itinerary-package'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 4. 14-Day Grand Tour Package
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'The complete Sri Lankan itinerary. Fourteen days covering the Cultural Triangle, Anuradhapura, Kandy, tea country, Ella, Yala safari, and multiple south coast beaches. No compromises.',
    intro = 'If you have two weeks, you can do Sri Lanka properly. Not just the greatest hits — you get Anuradhapura''s ancient stupas, two nights in Ella to hike and breathe, a dedicated wildlife day at Yala, and multiple beach stops along the south coast. This route gives you breathing room. Two nights at the cultural triangle instead of one. Two nights in Ella. Two or three nights on the coast. That means you''re not packing every single morning, which is the number one complaint from shorter trips. The extra days let you add experiences you''d otherwise skip: a cooking class in Kandy, a tea estate tour in Nuwara Eliya, sunrise at Pidurangala, a whale watching trip from Mirissa. Your private car is there the whole time, so you can change plans on the fly.',
    highlights = 'Anuradhapura at dawn when the dagobas cast long shadows across the grass and monks in orange robes walk between temples. The two-hour train from Nuwara Eliya to Ella through tunnels and tea plantations — book the observation car. A private safari at Yala where you spend 40 minutes tracking a leopard instead of the standard 10-minute stop. Doing nothing at a south coast beach hotel for an entire afternoon. The Galle Fort walking tour with a guide who knows which Dutch governor built which wall.',
    pricing_notes = 'Over 14 days, hotel costs add up. Budget USD 80-120/night for mid-range, USD 150-250 for nicer beach hotels the final 3 nights. Anuradhapura, Sigiriya, and Polonnaruwa entry fees: about USD 30 each per person. Yala safari jeep + park fees: USD 80-120 per person. The train ticket is cheap but hard to get — book through your hotel 2-4 weeks ahead. Whale watching: USD 40-60 per person. Cooking class: USD 20-30 per person. Meals: USD 5-20 per person depending on venue.',
    vehicle_options = 'A minivan is recommended for this length. You''ll accumulate luggage, souvenirs, and the inevitable bag of cinnamon and tea you buy at a roadside shop. The Anuradhapura-to-Sigiriya road is good; the Ella-to-Yala road is winding. A Toyota KDH or similar gives you the space and suspension for both.',
    cta_text = 'Got two weeks and want to see it all without feeling rushed? Send your dates and we''ll build a route with breathing room.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Is 14 days too long for Sri Lanka?","a":"For a first visit, it''s ideal. You cover every region without rushing. For a second visit, it lets you go deeper — more hiking, more beaches, more time in one place."},{"q":"What''s the best time for this route?","a":"December-April for the full circuit including south coast beaches. May-September still works well for the cultural triangle and hill country."},{"q":"Can I remove Anuradhapura?","a":"Yes. If ancient cities aren''t your thing, skip Anuradhapura and add an extra Yala safari or a Mirissa whale watching day instead."}]'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-14-day-grand-tour-package'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 5. Honeymoon Package
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'Built for two. Romantic hotels, private experiences, sunset dinners, and a pace that lets you actually be together. Tea country, safari, and a beach finish.',
    intro = 'A honeymoon in Sri Lanka works because the island gives you variety without long travel days. You get the intimacy of a jungle lodge with monkeys in the trees, the charm of a colonial tea bungalow with mountain views, and the indulgence of a beach resort where you don''t leave the room for a day. This route keeps stays to two or three nights minimum — no single-night checkouts. You''ll have a private vehicle with a driver who knows to disappear when you want privacy and appears when you need recommendations. The romantic moments are built in: sunset at Pidurangala with Sigiriya in the background, dinner on a Kandy lakefront terrace, a private safari jeep (no strangers sharing your vehicle), and a beach hotel where the staff remember your drink order.',
    highlights = 'Sunrise from Pidurangala — the less-crowded neighbour of Sigiriya with a killer view of the rock fortress. A private dinner in a Kandy hillside restaurant overlooking the lake. The Nuwara Eliya tea bungalow where you sip single-estate Ceylon on a veranda surrounded by tea bushes. Ella hike to Little Adam''s Peak — easy enough that you''re not sweating through your vacation wardrobe, scenic enough for the photos. A sunset walk along Galle Fort ramparts, just the two of you and the Indian Ocean. The beach resort where you do nothing for two entire days and love every minute.',
    pricing_notes = 'Honeymoon budgets vary wildly. Romantic boutique hotels: USD 150-400/night. The tea bungalow: USD 200-350/night. Beach resort: USD 200-500/night. Expect total accommodation for 10-12 nights at USD 2,000-5,000. Add private safari jeep (USD 120-180), private guides (USD 50-80 per site), and a few splurge dinners (USD 50-100 per couple). This is the trip where you pay for the room upgrade and don''t regret it.',
    vehicle_options = 'A clean, air-conditioned sedan is fine for two people. We recommend the upgrade to a newer model (Toyota Allion or similar) with leather seats and better AC — it sets the right tone for a honeymoon.',
    cta_text = 'Planning your honeymoon in Sri Lanka? Tell us your style and we''ll pick the romantic stays, the private experiences, and the quiet moments.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Is Sri Lanka good for a honeymoon?","a":"Yes — it''s one of the best value honeymoon destinations. You get variety (beach, hills, culture, wildlife) without the price tag of the Maldives or Bali."},{"q":"What if we want more beach time?","a":"We can extend the beach portion to 4-5 nights. Tangalle and Hiriketiya are quieter than Galle and perfect for honeymooners."},{"q":"Do we need a photographer?","a":"We can arrange a local photographer for a couple of hours at key spots — Sigiriya sunrise, Galle Fort, or your beach hotel. It''s worth it for the photos you''ll actually frame."}]'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-honeymoon-package'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 6. Family Tour Package
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'Designed for families — shorter drives, fewer hotel changes, kid-friendly activities, and a pace that won''t trigger a meltdown.',
    intro = 'Family travel in Sri Lanka works best when you accept the limits. Kids can''t handle 5-hour drives. They won''t appreciate Sigiriya after the 500th step. They need pools, short activities, and meals at reasonable hours. This route respects all of that. We keep drives to 2-3 hours max. We pick hotels with pools — actually good pools, not plunge pools. We schedule one major activity per day, usually in the morning before the heat hits, and leave afternoons for swimming, napping, or impromptu cricket on the beach. The route hits Sigiriya (do Pidurangala instead of the main rock if the kids are small), Kandy (the temple gardens are fun for running around), Udawalawe for elephants (every kid loves elephants), and a beach stretch where the whole family decompresses.',
    highlights = 'Watching your kids see their first elephant at Udawalawe — the genuine awe beats any theme park ride. The Elephant Transit Home where orphaned baby elephants are bottle-fed — open viewing times at 9am, noon, and 3pm. Pidurangala''s short, easy climb that ends with a view even parents love. A train ride from Kandy through tea country — kids love trains, and this stretch is genuinely scenic. The beach at Hiriketiya — a horseshoe bay with gentle waves perfect for children. The cinnamon island boat trip near Galle — 20 minutes, wildlife included.',
    pricing_notes = 'Families need bigger vehicles and often two connecting rooms or family suites. Budget USD 100-200/night for accommodation. Safari jeeps are cheaper per person shared (USD 50-70 per person). Kids under 6 often get free or half-price entry at attractions. The Elephant Transit Home is about USD 10 per person. A larger vehicle (minivan) costs more than a sedan — factor in an extra USD 15-20 per day. Extra hotel room for older kids who refuse to share: USD 50-80/night.',
    vehicle_options = 'A minivan is essential for families. Toyota KDH or Nissan Urvan with 7-8 seats. Request car seats or boosters when booking if your kids are under 12. The extra space matters for snacks, toys, bags, and the inevitable car nap.',
    cta_text = 'Planning a Sri Lanka trip with kids? Tell us their ages and interests and we''ll build a route that keeps the whole family happy.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Is Sri Lanka safe for kids?","a":"Yes. Sri Lanka is very family-friendly. People love children here, and the tourist infrastructure is safe. The main risks are sunburn, mosquitoes, and street food that''s too spicy — all manageable."},{"q":"What age group works best?","a":"Ages 5-15 work great. Under 5 is doable but you''ll move slower. The logistics (car seats, nap schedules, picky eating) are harder with toddlers."},{"q":"Can we do the safari with young kids?","a":"Udawalawe is perfect for families — the elephants are easy to spot and kids can stay in the jeep for the 3-hour drive. Yala requires more patience and is better for older kids."}]'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-family-tour-package'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 7. Wildlife Safari Package (standalone, 5 days)
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'Short, focused wildlife itinerary for travellers who care more about animal sightings than temples. Yala, Udawalawe, and Bundala in five days.',
    intro = 'This is the condensed wildlife tour for travellers who want maximum animal time with minimum cultural filler. Five days, three parks, no temple visits unless you ask for them. You''ll hit Yala for leopards, Udawalawe for elephants, and Bundala for birdlife. The route keeps you near the park gates so you''re first in at dawn. The jeeps are private, so you control the pacing. The driver-tracker combo is experienced — they read the bush, know the animal patterns, and respect the wildlife. This isn''t the safari where the driver radios other jeeps and you end up in a scrum of 20 vehicles around a leopard in a tree. We avoid those operators.',
    highlights = 'Yala Block 1 at dawn — the best leopard territory in Sri Lanka, with sightings in 70% of morning drives during peak season. Udawalawe''s elephant herds — you''ll see 30-50 elephants in a single morning drive, guaranteed. Bundala''s wetlands in January-March when migratory birds from Siberia cover the lagoons. The Elephant Transit Home feeding sessions. Sundowners at your lodge with the sounds of the bush around you.',
    pricing_notes = 'Five days of wildlife-heavy travel means higher costs. Budget USD 300-450 per person for park fees and safari jeeps across 5 days. Accommodation near parks runs USD 80-180/night. A private jeep for the full safari can be arranged but costs more than day-by-day booking. Meals at park lodges are limited and pricier than city restaurants — budget USD 15-25 per meal.',
    vehicle_options = 'Your main vehicle is a sedan or minivan for airport-to-lodge transfers. Safari jeeps are arranged separately at each park. We recommend private jeeps throughout — they cost more (USD 120-180 vs 50-80 shared) but the flexibility is worth it for serious wildlife viewing.',
    cta_text = 'Focused wildlife safari for travellers who want leopards, elephants, and birds. Send your dates and we''ll pick the parks with the best current sightings.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"What are the best months for this tour?","a":"February-July for Yala leopards. June-September for Minneriya elephant gathering. Year-round for Udawalawe elephants. January-March for Bundala migratory birds."},{"q":"Can I extend to 7 days?","a":"Yes — add Minneriya and/or Wilpattu for a more complete wildlife circuit. Ask us for the extended version."},{"q":"Will I definitely see a leopard?","a":"Nothing is guaranteed in the wild, but Yala Block 1 has the highest leopard density in Asia. Our trackers have a 70%+ success rate in peak season."}]'
WHERE slug = 'sri-lanka-wildlife-safari-package'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 8. Luxury Sri Lanka Slow Travel Package
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE tours
SET excerpt = 'Premium Sri Lanka with fewer hotel changes, private guides, better rooms, and a pace designed for people who don''t do rushed.',
    intro = 'Luxury in Sri Lanka isn''t about gold taps. The real luxury here is solitude — a jungle lodge where your only neighbours are monkeys, a colonial tea bungalow with mountain mist rolling past your window, a safari camp with canvas walls and a copper bathtub, and a Galle Fort villa with a private plunge pool. This route strips out the filler and keeps you in the best properties for 2-3 nights each. You''ll have private guides at every cultural site, a premium vehicle with refreshments and WiFi, and experiences built for lingering rather than ticking boxes. The pace means you never check out after one night except for the airport arrival. Everything else is two or three nights. You''ll actually relax.',
    highlights = 'A private sunrise at Sigiriya with a guide who knows every carving — and when to stay quiet. Afternoon tea at a restored colonial bungalow in Hatton, single-estate Ceylon with mist rolling through the valley. A private safari jeep at Yala where your driver circles back when the tracker spots something. Dinner at a Galle Fort courtyard restaurant — candlelit, colonial arches, food that surprises you. A beach day where the hotel staff set up your sun lounger before you ask.',
    pricing_notes = 'This is the premium option. Budget USD 300-600/night for accommodation. Private guides: USD 80-120 per day. Private safari jeep: USD 150-200. Premium vehicle upgrade (Toyota KDH or similar): USD 30-50/day extra. Expect total accommodation for 10-11 nights at USD 3,500-6,500. Meals at boutique properties: USD 50-100 per person for dinner with drinks.',
    vehicle_options = 'A premium vehicle with leather seats, extra legroom, bluetooth audio, and a cooler stocked with water and soft drinks. Toyota KDH or equivalent. The suspension matters on the winding roads — this isn''t the budget sedan.',
    cta_text = 'Want Sri Lanka done properly — private guides, premium stays, zero compromises? Tell us your travel style and we''ll design a luxury route worth every cent.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"How far ahead should I book?","a":"3-6 months for the best properties, especially November-April. The tea bungalows and safari lodges book out far in advance."},{"q":"Can I add a private chef?","a":"Some villa properties offer private chef service. We can arrange it at certain beach hotels and the Galle Fort villa. Ask when booking."},{"q":"What about a photographer?","a":"We can arrange a private photographer for a half-day or full-day shoot. Popular for honeymooners and milestone trips."}]'
WHERE slug = 'luxury-sri-lanka-slow-travel-package'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;
